course: dictionary
lesson: 8501
title: Sarcasm: saying the opposite
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sarcasm is saying the opposite of what you mean, often in a mocking voice, to make fun of something or to show you are annoyed. To spot it, check whether the words fit the situation and the tone. If it is pouring on sports day and someone says «Oh, great!», the words do not fit, so it is probably sarcasm.
explain: Writers use sarcasm to show how a character feels. The words say one thing, but the voice and the situation say another. A flat, bored voice with excited words is a clue. Ask yourself: does what they say match what is really happening?
explain: Sarcasm is different from making things bigger or smaller. «We'll be here till we're ninety» makes a wait sound longer (hyperbole). «Bit of a wait, eh» makes it sound shorter (understatement). Sarcasm says the opposite: «Oh brilliant, I LOVE standing in the rain.»
explain: In texts there is no voice or face, so sarcasm is easy to miss. Tags like /s (sarcastic) and /j (joking), or an emoji, can help. If a friend misses your sarcasm, explain kindly. Sarcasm can hurt feelings, so think about who you are talking to.
fact: Sarcasm means saying the opposite of what you mean.
fact: Sarcasm is often said in a mocking voice.
fact: People use sarcasm to make fun of something or to show they are annoyed.
fact: In texts, tone tags like /s and /j, or emoji, can show what you really mean.
fact: Sarcasm can hurt feelings, so use it with care.
exercise: It pours on sports day and someone says «Oh, great!» in a flat voice. What is this? | Understatement, making it smaller | *Sarcasm | Hyperbole, making it bigger | A real compliment
exercise: «Oh brilliant, I LOVE standing in the rain.» What does the speaker really mean? | They love standing in the rain | They are very happy about the weather | *They hate standing in the rain | They want a rain jacket
exercise: Which sentence makes something BIGGER rather than saying the opposite? | *«We'll be here till we're ninety» | «Bit of a wait, eh» | «I suppose that was fine» | «Oh, lovely, a test»
test: Which word means saying the opposite of what you mean to tease someone or to show you are annoyed? | *Sarcasm | Hyperbole | Context | Synonym
test: After groaning, a friend says «Lovely, another spelling test» in a bored voice. What is most likely? | They are very excited about the test | They are telling a joke that is funny | They are asking for help with spelling | *They are annoyed and being sarcastic
exam: Which clue most suggests sarcasm in a text message? | A plain sentence with no tone | A long list of facts | A word in capital letters used to name a place | *A tag like /s after a message that says something positive
